<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haghpat Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded c. 976</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haghpat Monastery is a medieval Armenian monastery in the Lori region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrovanuysh">Khosrovanuysh</a>, wife of King Ashot III, founded it around 976, and it flourished under the Kiurikian kings of Lori as a centre of learning with a large scriptorium. Together with nearby Sanahin it has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haghpat-monastery.jpg" alt="Haghpat Monastery" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yerevantsi · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haghpat_aerial_2023.3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Haghpat Monastery, also called Haghpatavank, was built between the 10th and 13th centuries. It stands on a fertile promontory in the Javakheti Range, partway up a hillside overlooking the Debed River in the Lori region. The site was chosen to give protection and concealment while remaining inconspicuous, and the monastery is surrounded by many hamlets. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanahin-monastery">Sanahin Monastery</a> nearby was built around the same time.</p><p>The largest church, the Cathedral of Surb Nshan, was probably begun in 967-976 by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrovanuysh">Khosrovanuysh</a> and was completed in 991 by King Smbat II. It is a typical example of tenth-century Armenian architecture: its central dome rests on four large pillars of the lateral walls, and the outer walls are dotted with triangular recesses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A fresco in the apse shows Christ Pantocrator. Its donor, the Armenian Prince Khutulukhaga, is depicted in the south transept, and the founder's sons, Princes Smbat and Kurike, appear with Queen Khosravanuysh in a bas-relief on the east gable.</p><p>Further buildings followed over the centuries. The small domed Church of Sourb Grigor dates from 1005. Two side chapels were added to the original church, the larger at the beginning of the 13th century and the smaller, known as the Hamazasp House, in 1257. A three-storey free-standing belltower was built in 1245. Other additions of the 13th century include the chapel of Sourb Astvatsatsin, a library and scriptorium, later converted into a storeroom, and a large refectory outside the monastery limits. Khachkars, or cross-stones, of the 11th to 13th centuries stand in the grounds; the best known is the Amenaprkich, or All-Savior, khachkar, which has stood since 1273.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1260 Prosh Khaghbakian led Georgian and Armenian forces with his Zakarid suzerains Avag and Shahnshah to support the smaller Mongol force of Hulegu at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a>. When the city was captured after a siege of two years, the Muslims were massacred and the Christians spared. Christian relics were brought back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, many to Haghpat, which received the hand of the Apostle Bartholomew.</p><p>The monastery has been damaged many times. An earthquake around 1130 destroyed parts of it, and it was not restored for fifty years. It was attacked by armed forces over the centuries and damaged by another earthquake in 1988, yet much of the complex stands without substantial alteration. In 1996 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> placed Haghpat and Sanahin on the World Heritage List, describing them as a masterpiece of religious architecture and a major centre of learning in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, the highest flowering of Armenian religious architecture, which blended Byzantine ecclesiastical and Caucasian vernacular styles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haghpat Monastery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The royal family endowed the monasteries of Sanahin and <b>Haghpat</b>, founded by Queen Khosrovanuysh in the 960s and 970s, which became major centres of learning and manuscript copying.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lori">Kingdom of Lori</a></li></ul></section>
